austria germany and the netherlands are  all seeing surges in coveted infections  and deaths so why isn't that happening  here and can the government really be  confident in sticking to plan a  well a quick glance at the numbers and  you can see why those countries are  considering lockdowns rocketing case  numbers in the netherlands here in dark  blue austria in grey and lightview  germany is now about level with the uk  but cases are rising fast so what's  going on well vaccines could have a lot  to do with it as you can see here the  uk's uptake was faster and started  earlier germany and austria only reached  our levels of coverage in recent weeks  there was also a shorter gap between  doses for much of these countries  rollouts combined that could translate  into lower levels of protection overall  on top of that here we started booster  vaccines over two months ago and the  over 12 started getting jabs last month  the more people you vaccinate the fewer  there are to infect  but timing is probably crucial too let's  look at cases again  infection rates have been kept low in  many parts of europe for a combination  of restrictions through late summer and  autumn in the uk we opened up in july  and we stayed open we're not seeing this  late autumn wave because we've had high  case numbers for months  over the summer months when we were  experiencing a big surge driven by the  more transmissible delta variant they  had very low numbers of cases  we have as a consequence of that wave  over the summer a lot higher natural  immunity and superimpose on that natural  immunity obviously is vaccine-induced  immunity  there's certainly evidence for that look  at covid antibodies in 16 to 24 year  olds in england up at around 96 percent  yet only 84 have had at least one dose  of the vaccine compelling evidence of  natural infection topping up vaccine  protection  so is this an indication of the uk  strategy well let's look at deaths with  a covert infection you can certainly  argue we've avoided a winter surge so  far compared with others but at what  cost for months the uk that's in red has  been averaging 100 to 150 covered deaths  a day since we opened up on july 19th  nearly 15 000 people have died with  covet  on top of that there's the impact of  severe illness and the drain it's  putting on the nhs we're sticking to  plan a but there are many cold winter  months still to come
 

